@page "/HowItWorks/dividends"
@using InvestmentTaxCalculator.Components

<HowItWorksGuideLayout PageAddress="HowItWorks/dividends">
    <ChildContent>
        <div class="section">
            <h2 class="section-title">Types of dividend entry</h2>
            <div class="table-responsive">
                <table class="table table-sm">
                    <thead>
                        <tr><th>Type</th><th>How the app uses it</th></tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>Dividend</td><td>Taxable dividend income</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Payment in lieu of dividend</td><td>Taxable dividend income</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Excess Reportable Income (Dividend)</td><td>Taxable dividend income, added from the <a href="HowItWorks/reporting-funds" class="link-styled">ERI and Equalisation</a> page</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Withholding tax</td><td>Foreign tax paid, shown separately against the dividends from the same country</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Return of capital</td><td>Not dividend income. It reduces the cost of the holding instead (see <a href="HowItWorks/return-of-capital" class="link-styled">Return of capital</a>)</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
            <p>
                The <a href="DividendYearlyTaxSummaryPage" class="link-styled">Dividend/Income Summary</a> totals these by tax
                year and by country, and the <a href="DividendDataPage" class="link-styled">Dividend/Income Data</a> page lists
                every entry.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="section">
            <h2 class="section-title">Which country a dividend comes from</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    The country is taken from the first two letters of the share's ISIN and matched against HMRC's list of
                    countries. For Interactive Brokers, withholding tax entries described as "US TAX" or "CA TAX" are set to
                    the US or Canada.
                </li>
                <li>
                    The ISIN can be out of date in old statements, or its country may not be on HMRC's list. You then get a
                    warning and the dividend is listed under an unknown region.
                </li>
                <li>Dividends imported from Interactive Investor have no ISIN, so they are always listed under an unknown region.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="section">
            <h2 class="section-title">Other rules</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    Only dividends received while you are UK resident are counted. Dividends in periods you mark as
                    non-resident or temporary non-resident on the <a href="HowItWorks/residency" class="link-styled">Residency
                    Status</a> page are left out.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Some fund distributions, such as those from bond funds, are taxed as interest instead of dividends. On the
                    <a href="ConvertPage" class="link-styled">Income taxable dividend selection</a> page you can choose
                    tickers whose dividends are treated as "ETF dividend income", which is counted with interest.
                </li>
                <li>
                    If a distribution includes equalisation, adding the equalisation on the ERI and Equalisation page reduces
                    that dividend by the equalisation amount.
                </li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </ChildContent>
    <FurtherReading>
        <li><a href="HowItWorks/interest" class="link-styled">Interest and bond income</a></li>
        <li><a href="HowItWorks/reporting-funds" class="link-styled">Excess reportable income and equalisation</a></li>
    </FurtherReading>
</HowItWorksGuideLayout>
